  <title>5.12.4: Possible Equation for a Function on a Graph</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01457</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.12.4: Possible Equation for a Function on a Graph</md:title>
    <md:uuid>4c4f7781-f093-493c-9199-0e3f50a133fd</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>
<title>Student Activity</title>



<para>Examine the three graphs representing three exponential functions: \(f\), \(g\), and \(h\).</para>
<para><figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/c91699a5862502edaffc0fc9cafd5d89472eebad"/></media></figure></para>

<para>The functions \(f\) and \(h\) are defined as:</para>
<list>
<item>\(f(x)=10 \cdot 2^x\)</item>
<item>\(h(x)=20 \cdot 4^x\)</item>
</list>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item>Which of the following could define function \(g\)? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</item>
  </list>

<list>
    <item>\(g(x)=20 \cdot (1.5)^x\)</item>
    <item>\(g(x)=20 \cdot (2.5)^x\)</item>
    <item>\(g(x)=10 \cdot (3.5)^x\)</item>
    <item>\(g(x)=20 \cdot (4.5)^x\)</item>
</list>

<para> <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<para>\(g(x)=20 \cdot (2.5)^x\)
</para>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item>Explain your reasoning.</item>
</list>

<para> <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<para>Equation B. The graph of \(g\) has the same \(y\)-intercept as the graph of \(h\), which is 20. It grows more quickly than \(f\) but more slowly than \(h\), so the growth factor must be greater than 2 but less than 4.</para>



<title>Response to Student Thinking</title>
<title> More Chances</title>
<para>The warm up in Lesson 13 provides an opportunity to discuss the ideas in this cool-down. Use the information you get about student thinking from this cool-down to guide the synthesis of the warm up in Lesson 13.</para></section>
</content>
